I've finished building all four phases. Typecheck (server and frontend), lint, and the 37 new or affected unit tests all pass. It has not been
  run end-to-end: no dev server, no upgrade command against a real workspace, and no live data centre. Nothing is committed.

  The last session ran out of memory during nx typecheck twenty-server, because it runs typecheck tasks in parallel next to VS Code's TypeScript
  server and Jest. I typechecked each package one at a time with a memory cap instead, and saved that recipe to memory.

  What you get
  - Record pages: a "Database" tab on Company, Person, Opportunity, Note, Task and every custom object. It lists attached records with a preview,
    a status badge, and View / Refresh / Remove / open-in-data-centre actions. The Attach button opens a two-pane picker (databases and tables on
    the left, searchable paginated records on the right), limited to 10 attachments per record.
  - Settings → Data Centre: URL, a write-only API token (only the last 4 characters are shown), an enable toggle, and a Test connection button.
    It's visible only with the new DATABASE_CENTRE_INTEGRATION permission.
  - Background: stale previews refresh hourly, and records deleted or made inaccessible upstream get flagged. Metadata lookups are cached, and
    outbound calls are capped at 120 per minute per workspace.
  - Security: the token is encrypted and included in secret-encryption:rotate. Outbound calls use the existing SSRF-protected HTTP client, never
    follow redirects, and cap responses at 5 MB.
  - Upgrades:
    - New workspaces get everything automatically, including new custom objects.
    - For existing workspaces I added one database migration for the new tab type and two upgrade commands: one adds the new objects, tabs and
      permission, the other adds the relation and tab to existing custom objects.

  Deviations from the design
  - databaseConnection is locked out of the generic record API. Without that, anyone with ordinary edit rights on that object could point the URL
    at their own server and receive the decrypted token. It can now only be changed from Settings.
  - The upgrade commands are in version 2.22, not 2.23. A 2.22 migration would never run on an instance that has already upgraded past that point,
    so the first upgrade command also adds the new tab type to the database itself (idempotent). 2.23 commands would stay inactive until you bump
    the version.
  - Browsing is two levels, not three: databases grouped by space, then tables. That's what the data centre API your other project already uses
    supports.
  - Dashboard and Workflow can hold attachments but don't get the tab: Dashboard has no record page layout, and a Database tab doesn't fit
    Workflow's layout.
  - No custom audit log: attach and remove go through the normal record-saving path, like Notes and Files, and don't write anything extra.
  - Frontend GraphQL types were written by hand, because generating them needs a running server. Run npx nx run twenty-front:graphql:generate
    --configuration=metadata once the server is up.

  To try it
  1. Enable IS_DATABASE_CENTRE_INTEGRATION_ENABLED for a workspace.
  2. Run yarn command:prod upgrade so existing workspaces get the new objects and tabs.
  3. Give a role the "Data Centre" permission, then configure the connection and click Test connection.
  4. Run cron:register:all to start the hourly refresh.

  I'd test the two upgrade commands on a copy of the database before running them in production.
